NAME: MIKE SIMS
OPERATORS: K4GMH
SOAPBOX: Thanks to URE for sponsoring the Contest. Thanks 
SOAPBOX: also to all who were kind enough to work me.
SOAPBOX: Conditions were weird on this end.  Had to take a 
SOAPBOX: break for several hours, 2000 - 2400z, which 
SOAPBOX: strongly suggested to move one of the SO2R positions 
SOAPBOX: to 40 meters.  Turns out 40 meters, along with 80 meters
SOAPBOX: had strong QRN from early Spring storms in the 
SOAPBOX: South USA.  The QRN, even with directional antennas,
SOAPBOX: was too strong to tame for a less than tense operating
SOAPBOX: on 40 - 80 meters.  Finally called it quits at 0300z and 
SOAPBOX: watch the end of the Kentucky/Wisconsin basketball
SOAPBOX: game.  However, turns out I should have stuck with it as
SOAPBOX: it got worse the next morning on the upper HF bands:>(
SOAPBOX: 
SOAPBOX: Tried to get back on 1130z and found I had a bad connection on one of
SOAPBOX: the HB filter switching box connectors.  Took half hour 
SOAPBOX: to find it (the crawling under the operating desk was
SOAPBOX: the hard part) and get back to operating in the morning (1200z).
SOAPBOX: Started listening to 15 and 20 meters.  Twenty had some 
SOAPBOX: European stations coming thru, but 15 meters was a blank!
SOAPBOX: The day before 15 m had a number of strong European RTTY 
SOAPBOX: signals at 1200z even though the Contest hadn't started.  
SOAPBOX: What was wrong?  A few minutes later learned there had
SOAPBOX: been a large Solar flare(?)and this probably was the
SOAPBOX: reason for the loss of propagation to Europe (SA stations were still
SOAPBOX: being heard).  Later in the morning, some strong signals from Europe (SA 
SOAPBOX: stations seemed to get stronger as well)on 15 m and 20 m.  Fifteen 
SOAPBOX: meters propagation became good to point most  
SOAPBOX: Asian stations were stronger than many of the European 
SOAPBOX: signals.  
SOAPBOX: 
SOAPBOX: Good participation by the EA operators.  Nice to see all 
SOAPBOX: the EA activity on the various bands.  
SOAPBOX: 
SOAPBOX:   73,
SOAPBOX:   Mike, K4GMH
